[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Basic Mappings, Table Strategies, and Column Configurations in JPA"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Java Persistence API (JPA) provides a robust framework for mapping Java objects to relational database tables. Understanding basic mappings, table strategies, and column configurations is essential for building scalable and maintainable enterprise applications."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Entity Mappings"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"To map a Java class to a database table, you use the @Entity annotation. The @Id annotation specifies the primary key of the entity, while @GeneratedValue defines the generation strategy for that primary key."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
@Table(name = "users")
public class User {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(name = "username", nullable = false, unique = true, length = 50)
    private String username;

    // Getters and Setters
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Advanced Column Configurations"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The @Column annotation allows fine-grained control over how entity fields map to database columns. Key attributes include:"},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"name: Specifies the column name in the database."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"nullable: Determines if the column accepts null values (default is true)."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"unique: Ensures all values in the column are unique."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"length: Defines the column length for string types (default is 255)."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"insertable and updatable: Controls whether the column is included in SQL INSERT and UPDATE statements."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Table Generation Strategies"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"JPA supports multiple primary key generation strategies via GenerationType. Selecting the correct strategy depends on your database vendor and architecture requirements:"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"IDENTITY: Relies on an auto-incrementing database column. Best for MySQL and PostgreSQL."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"SEQUENCE: Uses database sequences to generate unique IDs. Highly efficient, supported by Oracle and PostgreSQL."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"TABLE: Uses a separate database table to simulate a sequence. Highly portable across databases but can become a performance bottleneck."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"AUTO: Leaves the strategy selection up to the JPA provider (Hibernate)."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"By correctly applying entity annotations, configuring column constraints, and selecting optimal table generation strategies, you ensure data integrity and high performance across your persistence layer."}]
